  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 215 AM Wednesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Daily scattered showers and thunderstorms are \nexpected to continue for the next several days, mainly each \nafternoon through evening, with the better chances in our NW. But a \ngeneral decrease in coverage is expected this weekend through early \nnext week.\n\nThe weakening mid level low now over far N GA will continue a slow \nnorthward drift into E KY today, then track NE and dissipate, as \nbroad northern stream troughing over the N Plains and Upper Midwest \nearly this morning shifts E toward the Great Lakes. The result will \nbe a weak baggy shear axis holding just to our W through tonight, on \nthe W side of a strengthening anticyclone off the Mid Atlantic \ncoast. At the surface, a weak and nearly stationary frontal zone \nwill remain just to our W and N, along the western edge of the \nencroaching ridge to our E, leaving us within an otherwise ill-\ndefined surface pattern with light winds amidst a loose MSLP \npressure pattern. We'll have one more day of high PWs, up to 125% of \nnormal, so expect at least scattered coverage of showers and storms, \nwith coverage peaking mid afternoon through early evening. Morning \nclouds will once again be slow to lift and break up, but afternoon \ncloud breaks will allow for moderate CAPE up to 1500-2000 J/kg, \nalong with deep layer bulk shear a bit higher than on Tue but still \nonly around 20 kt. A few heavy downpours will remain possible today, \nalthough the slowly waning PW and uptick in cell motion should keep \nthis threat a little lower than yesterday and focused more on the \nwestern half. \n\nThe weak flow pattern will hold through late week as the offshore \nridge continues to build, resulting in gradually rising heights over \nNC, especially in the E. The mid level flow will increase slightly \nfrom the SW the WSW over the weekend as the broad Great Lakes trough \nshifts slowly E and weakens over the Mid Atlantic region, bringing \nthe aforementioned shear axis into our NW sections. Near-climo \nchance pops are likely to continue through late week and into the \nweekend, focused primarily in the NW (near the shear axis aloft with \nmore favorable mid level lapse rates) and SE (from sea breeze \naction). The retrograding ridge is expected to flatten and build \nover FL/GA/SC early next week as the broad northern stream trough \nweakens further over the Mid Atlantic/Northeast, shunting the shear \naxis well to our west and resulting in a weak W flow while surface \nlee troughing develops over the Piedmont. This should produce an \noverall downturn in pops early to mid portion of next week, with \nnear to below normal PW, lowering mid level lapse rates, and a weak \nmid level flow. Temps will not deviate more than a few degrees from \nnormal until Sun or Mon onward, when highs should trend to the lower \nto mid 90s with lows generally 70-75.",
